=== OBSERVATION (out of scope for the verdict, reported per the contract's "anything that
    surprised you, including behaviour no item asked about") ===
Operator attempted to set altNileBin by PASTING the path into the Settings text field.
Two distinct defects surfaced, NEITHER caused by Phase 34.9 (both live in generic UI
components untouched by this phase; 34.9 changed runner packaging, not input handling):

D1. Paste inserted a repeating unrenderable glyph ("infinite number of [square]"), not the
    clipboard text. Consistent with this project's standing finding that clipboard access
    under the Tauri/WKWebView host does not behave like Chromium
    (see memory: navigator.clipboard silently no-ops; window.api.clipboard{Write,Read}Text
    is the working channel). Not diagnosed further here -- doing so would be scope creep
    during a gate run, and no scored item depends on it.

D2. Pressing ENTER in the field does nothing. This is BY DESIGN and is the more likely
    trap for a real user: src/frontend/components/UI/PathSelectionBox/index.tsx commits via
      onBlur={(e) => onPathChange(e.target.value)}
    with no onKeyDown/Enter handler anywhere in PathSelectionBox ->
    TextInputWithIconField -> TextInputField. A typed or pasted path is therefore discarded
    unless the user happens to click away from the field. There is no affordance telling
    them that.

Item 5 proceeds via the folder-picker icon instead, which is the intended affordance:
handleIconClick() -> window.api.openDialog() -> onPathChange(selectedPath) directly,
bypassing the text field, the clipboard and the blur-commit. This does not weaken item 5 --
the item tests whether getNileBin()'s settings?.altNileBin branch takes priority over
archSpecificBinary(), which is unaffected by how the setting got its value.
